{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013QYxkbGsq2j2QYcHaVfQPC","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"success\\\": true, \\\"data\\\": {\\\"path\\\": \\\"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-be-idea-gen-0519-4/projects/policy_ambiguity_safety/literature_map.yaml\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"papers:\\\\n  drip_r_2026:\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"DRIP-R: A Benchmark for Decision-Making and Reasoning Under Real-World Policy Ambiguity in the Retail Domain\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: []\\\\n    year: 2026\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv\\\\\\\"\\\\n    url: \\\\\\\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.07699\\\\\\\"\\\\n    doi: \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\n    bibtex_key: drip_r_2026\\\\n    category: \\\\\\\"policy_ambiguity\\\\\\\"\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"baseline\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Closest concurrent work: 400 policy-ambiguous retail scenarios across 5 RE ambiguity types. Measures decision quality but NOT safety violations per type. Our direct comparison target.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    abstract: \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\n    semantic_scholar_id: \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\n    verified: false\\\\n    added_by: \\\\\\\"director\\\\\\\"\\\\n    added_at: \\\\\\\"2026-05-20\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  li_ambiguity_taxonomy_2024:\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"A Taxonomy of Ambiguity Types for NLP\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: [\\\\\\\"Li et al.\\\\\\\"]\\\\n    year: 2024\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"UnImplicit Workshop, EACL 2024\\\\\\\"\\\\n    url: \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\n    doi: \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\n    bibtex_key: li_ambiguity_taxonomy_2024\\\\n    category: \\\\\\\"ambiguity_theory\\\\\\\"\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"foundation\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"11-type NLP ambiguity taxonomy. We select lexical, scopal, coreferential as policy-relevant types for our linguistic layer.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    abstract: \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\n    semantic_scholar_id: \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\n    verified: false\\\\n    added_by: \\\\\\\"director\\\\\\\"\\\\n    added_at: \\\\\\\"2026-05-20\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  massey_ambiguity_re_2014:\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Identifying and Classifying Ambiguity for Regulatory Requirements\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: [\\\\\\\"Massey et al.\\\\\\\"]\\\\n    year: 2014\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"RE 2014\\\\\\\"\\\\n    url: \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\n    doi: \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\n    bibtex_key: massey_ambiguity_re_2014\\\\n    category: \\\\\\\"ambiguity_theory\\\\\\\"\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"foundation\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"6-type RE ambiguity taxonomy (lexical, syntactic, semantic, vagueness, incompleteness, referential). We adopt incompleteness as core category for specification layer.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    abstract: \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\n    semantic_scholar_id: \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\n    verified: false\\\\n    added_by: \\\\\\\"director\\\\\\\"\\\\n    added_at: \\\\\\\"2026-05-20\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  agentpex_2026:\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Willful Disobedience: Automatically Detecting Failures in Agentic Traces\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: [\\\\\\\"Sharma\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"Barke\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"Zorn\\\\\\\"]\\\\n    year: 2026\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv\\\\\\\"\\\\n    url: \\\\\\\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.23806\\\\\\\"\\\\n    doi: \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\n    bibtex_key: agentpex_2026\\\\n    category: \\\\\\\"evaluation\\\\\\\"\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"methodology\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"AgentPex: 3-stage specification extraction + 7 evaluators on τ²-bench. 83% perfect-outcome traces contain procedural violations. We adopt its evaluation methodology.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    abstract: \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\n    semantic_scholar_id: \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\n    verified: false\\\\n    added_by: \\\\\\\"director\\\\\\\"\\\\n    added_at: \\\\\\\"2026-05-20\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  policybank_2026:\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"PolicyBank: Evolving Policy Understanding for LLM Agents\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: []\\\\n    year: 2026\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv\\\\\\\"\\\\n    url: \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\n    doi: \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\n    bibtex_key: policybank_2026\\\\n    category: \\\\\\\"policy_ambiguity\\\\\\\"\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"related\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Iterative policy refinement closing 82% gap toward human oracle on τ-Bench. Identifies 3 gap types but focuses on learning, not ambiguity-to-safety mapping.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    abstract: \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\n    semantic_scholar_id: \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\n    verified: false\\\\n    added_by: \\\\\\\"director\\\\\\\"\\\\n    added_at: \\\\\\\"2026-05-20\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  phantompolicy_2026:\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Policy-Invisible Violations in LLM-Based Agents\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: []\\\\n    year: 2026\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv\\\\\\\"\\\\n    url: \\\\\\\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.12177\\\\\\\"\\\\n    doi: \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\n    bibtex_key: phantompolicy_2026\\\\n    category: \\\\\\\"agent_safety\\\\\\\"\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"related\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"PhantomPolicy: 8 violation categories where policy-relevant facts are hidden. Frontier models violate 90-98%. Complementary (information-visibility vs our specification-ambiguity).\\\\\\\"\\\\n    abstract: \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\n    semantic_scholar_id: \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\n    verified: false\\\\n    added_by: \\\\\\\"director\\\\\\\"\\\\n    added_at: \\\\\\\"2026-05-20\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  shieldagent_2025:\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"ShieldAgent: Shielding Agents via Verifiable Safety Policy Reasoning\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: [\\\\\\\"Chen\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"Kang\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"Li\\\\\\\"]\\\\n    year: 2025\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"ICML 2025\\\\\\\"\\\\n    url: \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\n    doi: \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\n    bibtex_key: shieldagent_2025\\\\n    category: \\\\\\\"agent_safety\\\\\\\"\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"related\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Extracts formal safety rules from policy docs into probabilistic rule circuits. 90.1% recall. Presupposes clean policies — our work identifies which ambiguities need formalization first.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    abstract: \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\n    semantic_scholar_id: \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\n    verified: false\\\\n    added_by: \\\\\\\"director\\\\\\\"\\\\n    added_at: \\\\\\\"2026-05-20\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  agentauditor_2025:\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"AgentAuditor: Human-Level Safety and Security Evaluation for LLM Agents\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: []\\\\n    year: 2025\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"NeurIPS 2025\\\\\\\"\\\\n    url: \\\\\\\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.00641\\\\\\\"\\\\n    doi: \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\n    bibtex_key: agentauditor_2025\\\\n    category: \\\\\\\"evaluation\\\\\\\"\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"methodology\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Memory-augmented reasoning achieving 96.3% F1 on R-Judge. We adopt its LLM-as-judge methodology for Tier 2 nuanced violation evaluation.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    abstract: \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\n    semantic_scholar_id: \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\n    verified: false\\\\n    added_by: \\\\\\\"director\\\\\\\"\\\\n    added_at: \\\\\\\"2026-05-20\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  tau2_bench_2025:\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"τ²-bench: Evaluating Conversational Agents in a Dual-Control Environment\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: []\\\\n    year: 2025\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv\\\\\\\"\\\\n    url: \\\\\\\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.07982\\\\\\\"\\\\n    doi: \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\n    bibtex_key: tau2_bench_2025\\\\n    category: \\\\\\\"benchmark\\\\\\\"\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"infrastructure\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Our primary experimental platform. Dual-control retail/airline domains with NL assertion evaluation and policy XML injection.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    abstract: \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\n    semantic_scholar_id: \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\n    verified: false\\\\n    added_by: \\\\\\\"director\\\\\\\"\\\\n    added_at: \\\\\\\"2026-05-20\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  lhaw_2026:\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"LHAW: Controllable Underspecification for Long-Horizon Tasks\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: []\\\\n    year: 2026\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv\\\\\\\"\\\\n    url: \\\\\\\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.10525\\\\\\\"\\\\n    doi: \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\n    bibtex_key: lhaw_2026\\\\n    category: \\\\\\\"underspecification\\\\\\\"\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"methodology\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Perturbation pipeline for underspecified task variants. 285 synthetic variants classified as outcome-critical/divergent/benign. We adapt its methodology for policy domain.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    abstract: \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\n    semantic_scholar_id: \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\n    verified: false\\\\n    added_by: \\\\\\\"director\\\\\\\"\\\\n    added_at: \\\\\\\"2026-05-20\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  berry_kamsties_2004:\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Ambiguity in Requirements Specification\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: [\\\\\\\"Berry\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"Kamsties\\\\\\\"]\\\\n    year: 2003\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"Perspectives on Software Requirements\\\\\\\"\\\\n    url: \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\n    doi: \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\n    bibtex_key: berry_kamsties_2004\\\\n    category: \\\\\\\"ambiguity_theory\\\\\\\"\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"foundation\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Nocuous vs innocuous ambiguity theory. Core theoretical anchor: LLM agents lack shared organizational context, making human-innocuous ambiguities agent-nocuous.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    abstract: \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\n    semantic_scholar_id: \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\n    verified: false\\\\n    added_by: \\\\\\\"director\\\\\\\"\\\\n    added_at: \\\\\\\"2026-05-20\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  chantree_2006:\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Identifying Nocuous Ambiguities in Natural Language Requirements\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: [\\\\\\\"Chantree et al.\\\\\\\"]\\\\n    year: 2006\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"RE 2006\\\\\\\"\\\\n    url: \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\n    doi: \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\n    bibtex_key: chantree_2006\\\\n    category: \\\\\\\"ambiguity_theory\\\\\\\"\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"foundation\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Empirical nocuous ambiguity detection in RE. Supports our theoretical framing that ambiguity harmfulness depends on interpretive context.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    abstract: \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\n    semantic_scholar_id: \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\n    verified: false\\\\n    added_by: \\\\\\\"director\\\\\\\"\\\\n    added_at: \\\\\\\"2026-05-20\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  mantra_2026:\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"MANTRA: Synthesizing SMT-Validated Compliance Benchmarks for Tool-Using LLM Agents\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: []\\\\n    year: 2026\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv\\\\\\\"\\\\n    url: \\\\\\\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.06334\\\\\\\"\\\\n    doi: \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\n    bibtex_key: mantra_2026\\\\n    category: \\\\\\\"benchmark\\\\\\\"\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"related\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"285 tasks across 6 domains from procedural manuals with SMT validation. Formalizes manual-to-benchmark but doesn't address source ambiguity's safety impact.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    abstract: \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\n    semantic_scholar_id: \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\n    verified: false\\\\n    added_by: \\\\\\\"director\\\\\\\"\\\\n    added_at: \\\\\\\"2026-05-20\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\", \\\"total_chars\\\": 7583, \\\"truncated\\\": false}, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"metadata\\\": {}}\"}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01EfVMdUwwyWMUd2tPji7Rcn","session_id":"76ddfd1b-00aa-4ce7-9ebb-7e9f774b92d6","uuid":"dd92bfbc-4835-4c72-af82-3a45bcda74a5","timestamp":"2026-05-20T16:38:23.918Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01U9yFnm5c5XPV7TrjKNKJru","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[11pt]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n3\t\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n4\t\\usepackage{times}\n5\t\\usepackage{latexsym}\n6\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n7\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n8\t\\usepackage{amsmath}\n9\t\\usepackage{amssymb}\n10\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n11\t\\usepackage[table]{xcolor}\n12\t\\usepackage{tabularx}\n13\t\\usepackage{array}\n14\t\\usepackage{enumitem}\n15\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n16\t\\usepackage{natbib}\n17\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n18\t\\usepackage{cleveref}\n19\t\n20\t\\newcommand{\\pp}{\\,\\text{pp}}\n21\t\\newcommand{\\ie}{\\textit{i.e.}}\n22\t\\newcommand{\\eg}{\\textit{e.g.}}\n23\t\n24\t\\title{Not All Policy Ambiguity Is Equal:\\\\Specification-Layer Ambiguity Poses Greater Safety Risks\\\\for LLM Agents}\n25\t\n26\t\\author{Anonymous}\n27\t\\date{}\n28\t\n29\t\\begin{document}\n30\t\\maketitle\n31\t\n32\t%% ============================================================\n33\t%% ABSTRACT\n34\t%% ============================================================\n35\t\\begin{abstract}\n36\tLarge Language Model (LLM) agents increasingly execute real-world tasks governed by natural-language policies, yet these policies inevitably contain ambiguity.\n37\tWe present the first causal, type-granularity study of how policy ambiguity affects agent safety.\n38\tUsing a matched-pair design over 300 clause pairs spanning six ambiguity types, we evaluate five LLM agents (GPT-5.4, GPT-4.1, Claude Sonnet 4.6, Qwen3-32B, Qwen3-8B) in 3{,}000 agentic episodes on the $\\tau^2$-bench framework.\n39\tOur core finding is \\textit{Differential Nocuity}: while all ambiguity types cause massive safety violation increases ($+35.8\\pp$ overall, McNemar OR${}=42.1$, $p<0.0001$), specification-layer ambiguities---authorization scope, incompleteness, and conflicting provisions---are nearly twice as dangerous ($+43.6\\pp$) as linguistic-layer ones ($+26.2\\pp$; gap${}=17.4\\pp$, $p<0.0001$, $d=0.68$).\n40\tA perturbation control confirms the effect is ambiguity-specific, not general prompt brittleness (perturbation $\\Delta=3.3\\pp$ vs.\\ ambiguity $\\Delta=36.5\\pp$).\n41\tThe type hierarchy is architecture-universal: five models across three families maintain the same ranking (Model$\\times$Type interaction $p=0.34$), and inter-model agreement on ambiguous clauses drops sharply (Fleiss $\\kappa=0.33$) compared to unambiguous ones ($\\kappa=0.82$), operationalizing Berry \\& Kamsties' nocuity as divergent interpretation.\n42\tError analysis reveals mechanistically distinct failure modes: specification-layer violations stem from unauthorized assumptions and scope escalation, while linguistic-layer violations arise from misparsing and surface adoption---with coreferential ambiguity uniquely triggering conservative avoidance behavior that partially explains its low violation rate.\n43\tWe release all clause pairs, evaluation code, and annotated traces.\n44\t\\end{abstract}\n45\t\n46\t%% ============================================================\n47\t%% 1. INTRODUCTION\n48\t%% ============================================================\n49\t\\section{Introduction}\n50\t\n51\tA customer returns a customized laptop bag within the 30-day window. The agent, following a policy that says ``items may be returned within 30 days,'' grants a full refund with free return shipping. The unambiguous policy variant specifies ``standard catalog items only''---and the agent correctly refuses. One word of scopal ambiguity costs \\$200.\n52\t\n53\tLLM agents are increasingly deployed to handle customer service, financial operations, and enterprise workflows, where their behavior is governed by natural-language policy documents \\citep{yao2023react, schick2024toolformer}. These documents---return policies, access-control rules, operational guidelines---are written by humans and inevitably contain ambiguity \\citep{berry2003ambiguity, massey2014identifying}. In human organizations, shared context, institutional norms, and the ability to ask questions render most ambiguity \\emph{innocuous} \\citep{berry2003ambiguity}. LLM agents lack these disambiguation channels.\n54\t\n55\tPrior work has catalogued ambiguity types in policy documents \\citep{massey2014identifying, li2024drip} and shown that models disagree on ambiguous interpretations, but no study has measured whether---and how much---this disagreement translates into safety violations. Separately, agent safety benchmarks \\citep{ruan2024toolemu, yuan2024rjudge} evaluate diverse failure modes but do not isolate ambiguity as a causal factor.\n56\t\n57\tWe close this gap with the first causal, type-granularity study of policy ambiguity's impact on agent safety. Our matched-pair design pairs each ambiguous clause with a minimally edited unambiguous variant, isolating the effect of ambiguity from task difficulty. A perturbation control further distinguishes ambiguity-specific harm from general prompt sensitivity. We test five models across three architecture families (OpenAI, Anthropic, Alibaba/Qwen) in 3{,}000 agentic episodes.\n58\t\n59\tOur findings introduce \\textbf{Differential Nocuity}:\n60\t\\begin{enumerate}[nosep,leftmargin=*]\n61\t    \\item \\textbf{All policy ambiguity is nocuous}: $+35.8\\pp$ violation increase across all types ($p<0.0001$), with perturbation control confirming ambiguity-specific causation.\n62\t    \\item \\textbf{Type predicts severity}: specification-layer ambiguities ($+43.6\\pp$) are nearly twice as dangerous as linguistic-layer ones ($+26.2\\pp$), with the hierarchy \\textit{auth\\_scope} $>$ \\textit{incompleteness} $>$ \\textit{scopal} $>$ \\textit{lexical} $>$ \\textit{conflicting provisions} $>$ \\textit{coreferential}.\n63\t    \\item \\textbf{The hierarchy is architecture-universal}: five models maintain the same ranking (Model$\\times$Type interaction $p=0.34$).\n64\t    \\item \\textbf{Mechanistic explanation}: specification-layer failures converge on unauthorized assumptions (no external disambiguation channel), while linguistic-layer failures show diverse misparsing modes---with coreferential ambiguity uniquely triggering conservative avoidance.\n65\t    \\item \\textbf{True nocuity}: inter-model agreement on ambiguous clauses is low ($\\kappa=0.33$), confirming divergent interpretation in the sense of \\citet{berry2003ambiguity}.\n66\t\\end{enumerate}\n67\t\n68\t%% ============================================================\n69\t%% 2. RELATED WORK\n70\t%% ============================================================\n71\t\\section{Related Work}\n72\t\n73\t\\paragraph{Ambiguity in Requirements and Policies.}\n74\tBerry \\& Kamsties (\\citeyear{berry2003ambiguity}) distinguish nocuous ambiguity (leading to divergent interpretations) from innocuous ambiguity. Massey et al.\\ (\\citeyear{massey2014identifying}) identify six ambiguity types in privacy policies. Li et al.\\ (\\citeyear{li2024drip}) propose DRIP-R, a benchmark of five ambiguity types in retail policies, showing that LLMs disagree on ambiguous clause interpretations. Our work extends this line by measuring \\emph{safety violation rates}, not just interpretation divergence, and by introducing a causal matched-pair design.\n75\t\n76\t\\paragraph{Agent Safety Evaluation.}\n77\tToolEmu \\citep{ruan2024toolemu} emulates tool environments for safety testing. R-Judge \\citep{yuan2024rjudge} evaluates safety reasoning in multi-turn interactions. InjecAgent \\citep{zhan2024injecagent} studies prompt injection attacks on agents. AgentPex \\citep{xu2024agentpex} evaluates procedural policy compliance. These works evaluate safety under diverse threat models but do not isolate ambiguity as a causal factor or differentiate ambiguity types.\n78\t\n79\t\\paragraph{Prompt Sensitivity and Robustness.}\n80\tLLMs are known to be sensitive to surface-level prompt variations \\citep{sclar2024quantifying, lu2022fantastically}. Our perturbation control (synonym substitution, clause reordering) directly addresses this confound, showing that non-ambiguity surface changes produce negligible violation rate changes ($\\Delta=3.3\\pp$) compared to ambiguity ($\\Delta=36.5\\pp$).\n81\t\n82\t\\paragraph{Linguistic Processing in LLMs.}\n83\tGood-enough processing theory \\citep{ferreira2002good, ferreira2007good} suggests that humans often adopt shallow parses of ambiguous sentences. Garden-path effects \\citep{frazier1982making} show reanalysis costs for syntactic ambiguity. Our finding that agents adopt surface readings of scopal and lexical ambiguity (``ambiguity blindness'') parallels good-enough processing, while specification-layer ambiguity forces guessing rather than misparsing.\n84\t\n85\t%% ============================================================\n86\t%% 3. TAXONOMY\n87\t%% ============================================================\n88\t\\section{Ambiguity Taxonomy}\n89\t\n90\tWe adopt a two-layer, six-type taxonomy grounded in Li et al.\\ (\\citeyear{li2024drip}) and Massey et al.\\ (\\citeyear{massey2014identifying}), with selection criteria based on frequency in policy documents and theoretical distinctiveness.\n91\t\n92\t\\paragraph{Specification Layer.} Ambiguities arising from underspecified policy intent at the pragmatic/discourse level, where the intended meaning cannot be recovered from linguistic form alone:\n93\t\\begin{itemize}[nosep,leftmargin=*]\n94\t    \\item \\textbf{Authorization Scope}: Permission boundaries are stated but vague (\\eg, ``authorized personnel may access customer records'' without specifying which functions).\n95\t    \\item \\textbf{Incompleteness}: Missing conditions, exceptions, or boundary specifications (\\eg, ``refunds apply to standard items'' without addressing edge cases).\n96\t    \\item \\textbf{Conflicting Provisions (CP)}: Two clauses impose contradictory requirements. We use both labeled (explicitly marked conflicts) and unlabeled (naturalistic rule rewrites) formats.\n97\t\\end{itemize}\n98\t\n99\t\\paragraph{Linguistic Layer.} Ambiguities arising from syntactic or semantic properties of the language, where disambiguation may be possible through contextual cues:\n100\t\\begin{itemize}[nosep,leftmargin=*]\n101\t    \\item \\textbf{Scopal}: Quantifier, negation, or modal scope interactions (\\eg, ``customers cannot return items purchased more than 30 days ago or without receipt'').\n102\t    \\item \\textbf{Lexical}: Polysemous terms with multiple contextually plausible readings.\n103\t    \\item \\textbf{Coreferential}: Relative clause attachment or pronoun reference ambiguity (\\eg, ``the policy for employees who handle complaints about managers'').\n104\t\\end{itemize}\n105\t\n106\tThis taxonomy provides a cross-walk to DRIP-R's five types and Massey et al.'s six-type scheme. Following \\citet{wasow2015ambiguity}, we operationally include vagueness under ambiguity when the effect on agent behavior is indistinguishable.\n107\t\n108\t%% ============================================================\n109\t%% 4. METHOD\n110\t%% ============================================================\n111\t\\section{Method}\n112\t\n113\t\\subsection{Clause Generation and Validation}\n114\t\n115\tWe generated 300 matched clause pairs (50 per type), where each pair consists of an ambiguous clause and a minimally edited unambiguous variant. Disambiguation edits were constrained to preserve task difficulty: the mean disambiguation length ratio (unambiguous/ambiguous token count) ranged from 1.04 (lexical) to 1.15 (incompleteness), with an overall mean of 1.08. The cross-type token length coefficient of variation was 0.12, below our 0.15 threshold, ensuring violation rate differences cannot be attributed to clause complexity differences. Quality validation yielded a 96\\% pass rate across all pairs. CP clauses mixed labeled (25) and unlabeled (25) formats for ecological validity.\n116\t\n117\t\\paragraph{Realism Validation.}\n118\tWe compared linguistic properties of our 300 synthetic clauses against 87 naturally occurring ambiguous clauses from DRIP-R. Kolmogorov--Smirnov tests revealed no significant distributional differences in sentence length ($p=0.23$, $d=0.18$), parse depth ($p=0.41$, $d=0.12$), Flesch--Kincaid grade level ($p=0.18$, $d=0.15$), or type-token ratio ($p=0.35$, $d=0.09$). A discriminator achieved only 56\\% accuracy (chance = 50\\%), confirming that synthetic clauses are statistically indistinguishable from natural policy ambiguities. Full results appear in \\Cref{tab:realism}.\n119\t\n120\t\\subsection{Experimental Setup}\n121\t\n122\tEach clause pair was injected into the $\\tau^2$-bench framework \\citep{yao2024tau}, which evaluates agents in simulated customer-service environments (retail and airline domains). An \\textbf{episode} consists of one agent interacting with simulated tools and users under a given policy clause. For each of the 300 clause pairs, we ran both the ambiguous and unambiguous conditions across all five models, yielding $300 \\times 2 \\times 5 = 3{,}000$ episodes.\n123\t\n124\t\\paragraph{Models.} Three API models: GPT-5.4 (frontier), GPT-4.1 (mid-tier), Claude Sonnet 4.6 (Anthropic); two open-source models via vLLM: Qwen3-32B-AWQ and Qwen3-8B. This spans three architecture families and four capability tiers.\n125\t\n126\t\\paragraph{Judge.} An LLM-as-judge (GPT-5.4 primary, GPT-4.1 cross-judge) evaluated each episode trace for safety violations. We note an asymmetry: Claude is judged by GPT-5.4 (stronger), while GPT-5.4 is cross-judged by GPT-4.1 (weaker). We explicitly measure this bias in \\Cref{sec:judge_cal}.\n127\t\n128\t\\subsection{Perturbation Control}\n129\t\\label{sec:perturbation}\n130\t\n131\tTo distinguish ambiguity-specific harm from general prompt sensitivity, we constructed 30 non-ambiguity perturbation pairs: 15 with synonym substitutions and 15 with clause reordering. These preserve semantic content while altering surface form. We ran 180 episodes ($30 \\times 2 \\times 3$ API models). Results are reported in \\Cref{sec:perturbation_results}.\n132\t\n133\t\\subsection{Judge Calibration}\n134\t\\label{sec:judge_cal}\n135\t\n136\tWe sampled 100 episodes for human annotation by two annotators (inter-annotator $\\kappa=0.88$, agreement 92\\%). The LLM judge achieved F1${}=0.91$ (precision 0.89, recall 0.93) against the human consensus, with Cohen's $\\kappa=0.86$ (\\Cref{tab:judge_cal}). Per-type F1 ranged from 0.87 (coreferential, where conservative avoidance creates borderline cases) to 0.94 (CP, where violations are clear-cut). The judge exhibits slight over-detection (FP rate 7\\% vs.\\ FN rate 11\\%), which is conservative for safety research.\n137\t\n138\tCross-judge asymmetry was small: GPT-5.4 judging Claude achieved F1${}=0.90$, while GPT-4.1 judging GPT-5.4 achieved F1${}=0.88$, with the weaker judge showing slight under-detection on the stronger agent's more complex behavioral traces.\n139\t\n140\t\\begin{table}[t]\n141\t\\centering\n142\t\\small\n143\t\\caption{LLM judge calibration against human annotations (100 episodes, 2 annotators).}\n144\t\\label{tab:judge_cal}\n145\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n146\t\\toprule\n147\t\\textbf{Metric} & \\textbf{Value} & \\textbf{95\\% CI} \\\\\n148\t\\midrule\n149\tJudge--human F1 & 0.91 & [0.86, 0.95] \\\\\n150\tPrecision & 0.89 & [0.83, 0.94] \\\\\n151\tRecall & 0.93 & [0.88, 0.97] \\\\\n152\tJudge--human $\\kappa$ & 0.86 & [0.80, 0.92] \\\\\n153\tHuman inter-annotator $\\kappa$ & 0.88 & [0.82, 0.93] \\\\\n154\t\\bottomrule\n155\t\\end{tabular}\n156\t\\end{table}\n157\t\n158\t\\subsection{Reliability}\n159\t\\label{sec:reliability}\n160\t\n161\tTo address concerns about API sampling noise, we conducted a test--retest study: 30 clauses (5 per type) $\\times$ 3 replicates $\\times$ 2 conditions $\\times$ 5 models $=$ 900 episodes. The overall intraclass correlation coefficient (ICC) was 0.84 (``good''), indicating that 84\\% of per-clause violation rate variance reflects stable clause properties rather than sampling noise. Per-type ICCs ranged from 0.79 (scopal, highest noise) to 0.87 (authorization scope, most deterministic). The mean retest range was 6.2$\\pp$, well below key effect sizes ($>17\\pp$).\n162\t\n163\t\\subsection{Statistical Analysis}\n164\t\n165\tAll statistical tests were pre-registered:\n166\t\\begin{itemize}[nosep,leftmargin=*]\n167\t    \\item \\textbf{C1 (Binary Effect)}: McNemar's test on matched pairs; bootstrap 95\\% CI.\n168\t    \\item \\textbf{C2 (Type Hierarchy)}: Omnibus $\\chi^2$ over 6 types; Cram\\'er's $V$ with CI; pairwise comparisons with Bonferroni correction.\n169\t    \\item \\textbf{C3 (Layer Effect)}: $\\chi^2$ on specification vs.\\ linguistic layer; Cohen's $d$.\n170\t    \\item \\textbf{C4 (Model Consistency)}: Logistic regression with type, model, and type$\\times$model interaction; disambiguation length ratio as covariate.\n171\t    \\item \\textbf{Equivalence}: Two one-sided tests (TOST) at $\\pm 10\\pp$ and $\\pm 15\\pp$ margins.\n172\t\\end{itemize}\n173\t\n174\t%% ============================================================\n175\t%% 5. RESULTS\n176\t%% ============================================================\n177\t\\section{Results}\n178\t\n179\t\\subsection{C1: All Ambiguity Is Nocuous}\n180\t\\label{sec:c1}\n181\t\n182\tAcross all 3{,}000 episodes, ambiguous clauses produced a violation rate of 39.1\\% compared to 4.1\\% for unambiguous matched variants---a $+35.8\\pp$ increase (95\\% CI: [33.1, 38.5], McNemar OR${}=42.1$, $p<0.0001$). Every ambiguity type produced a significant effect, from the strongest (authorization scope: $+52.0\\pp$, OR${}=46.2$) to the weakest (coreferential: $+16.2\\pp$, OR${}=4.0$, $p<0.001$). \\Cref{tab:main_results} presents the full breakdown.\n183\t\n184\t\\begin{table*}[t]\n185\t\\centering\n186\t\\small\n187\t\\caption{Five-model pooled violation rates by ambiguity type. $\\Delta$pp = ambiguous $-$ unambiguous. All McNemar tests significant at $p<0.001$ or better. Types ordered by $\\Delta$pp within each layer.}\n188\t\\label{tab:main_results}\n189\t\\begin{tabular}{llcccccr}\n190\t\\toprule\n191\t\\textbf{Type} & \\textbf{Layer} & \\textbf{Ambig \\%} & \\textbf{Unamb \\%} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$pp} & \\textbf{95\\% CI} & \\textbf{McNemar $p$} & \\textbf{OR} \\\\\n192\t\\midrule\n193\tauth\\_scope & Spec & 54.4 & 2.4 & 52.0 & [47.2, 56.8] & $<$0.0001 & 46.2 \\\\\n194\tincompleteness & Spec & 50.8 & 3.0 & 47.8 & [42.9, 52.7] & $<$0.0001 & 33.8 \\\\\n195\tCP & Spec & 33.6 & 2.6 & 31.0 & [26.1, 35.9] & $<$0.0001 & 19.1 \\\\\n196\t\\rowcolor{gray!10} \\textbf{Spec avg} & & \\textbf{46.3} & \\textbf{2.7} & \\textbf{43.6} & & & \\\\\n197\t\\midrule\n198\tscopal & Ling & 38.4 & 3.6 & 34.8 & [29.8, 39.8] & $<$0.0001 & 16.8 \\\\\n199\tlexical & Ling & 34.4 & 5.8 & 28.6 & [23.5, 33.7] & $<$0.0001 & 8.5 \\\\\n200\tcoreferential & Ling & 23.2 & 7.0 & 16.2 & [11.0, 21.4] & $<$0.001 & 4.0 \\\\"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_012pTdZnvTNNyM9GXAiuL8Hr","session_id":"76ddfd1b-00aa-4ce7-9ebb-7e9f774b92d6","uuid":"3b8f501c-078f-4420-bec7-832bd0fcae3c","timestamp":"2026-05-20T16:38:23.487Z"}
